hat your hair is less soft less  silky it also tangles more easily and it  doesn't hold on to hair conditioner as  well it also makes your hair more  hydrophilic or water-loving  and this means that it swells more when  it's wet and because much weaker  it also breaks up proteins in your hair  which makes the hair cuticle chip away  and this makes your hair dull and rough  but one of the biggest problems is  despite all this damage it still might  not completely work so it might not  break the dye molecules enough it still  absorbs light but now the light is  different and so you end up with weird  colors coming through the method I'm  using I found on the hair crazy website  it's called Katie's direct dye fading  treatment and I've linked it in the  description box before using the  treatment you should try to clarify your  hair and repair it as much as possible  so used a clarifying shampoo I used  olaplex to try to repair the bonds and I  avoided silicones in my hair products  for a few days right now my hair is like  this pale purplish color but the purple  just won't leave so what I'm gonna try  to do today is fade it down to a paler  shade that I can turn peach later on the  first step is to apply coconut oil to  dry hair and then wait for two hours if  anyone knows the methods they're getting  liquid coconut oil onto your hair  without it going everywhere I would love  it because it is literally dripping  everywhere right now and it's going to  be a pain to clean  coconut oil has saturated fatty acids  which means that its structure is skinny  enough to absorb between the cuticle  scales of your hair and this dissolves  up some of the dye molecules and gets  them out without any harshness I'm only  doing the treatment on half my hair so  we have a comparison to see how well it  fades alright so that's pretty saturated  now I'm just going to leave it for two  hours  you make a mix of 15% ammonia free  bleach powder 80% clarifying shampoo and  5% oils then you 